-- title:
--   Uniform bounded-step descent toward the apex inside a cone, along lattice points
-- statement:
--   Fix a dimension $d$ and an angle $\vartheta$ with $0 < \vartheta \le \pi/2$. Then there are constants $\delta > 0$ and $t_0 > 0$, depending only on $d$ and $\vartheta$, with the following property.
--
--   For every unit vector $v \in \mathbb{R}^d$, every aperture $\vartheta_V$ with $\vartheta \le \vartheta_V \le \pi/2$, every apex $c \in \mathbb{R}^d$, and every lattice point $x \in \mathbb{Z}^d$ lying in the cone $\tilde V(v, \vartheta_V)[c] = c + \tilde V(v,\vartheta_V)$ and satisfying $\lVert x - c\rVert > t_0$, there is another lattice point $y \in \mathbb{Z}^d$ with
--
--   $$y \in \tilde V(v,\vartheta_V)[c], \qquad \lVert y - c\rVert \le \lVert x - c\rVert - 1, \qquad \lVert y - x\rVert < \delta .$$
--
--   So from any lattice point sufficiently far out in the cone one can step, by a jump of length less than $\delta$, to another lattice point of the same cone that is at least one unit closer to the apex.
--
--   Iterating the step drives any far-out lattice point down to within distance $t_0$ of the apex in finitely many bounded-length jumps, all of them staying inside the cone; the essential feature is that $\delta$ and $t_0$ do not depend on the axis $v$, on the aperture $\vartheta_V \ge \vartheta$, or on the apex $c$.
--
--   **Formalization Note.** The quantifier order is what carries the uniformity: $\delta$ and $t_0$ are produced before $v$, $\vartheta_V$, $c$ and $x$ are introduced. The cone is the open one-sided cone of the configuration setting, and $\tilde V(v,\vartheta_V)[c]$ is `shift (cone v ϑV) c`.
